WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.382
Imagine a city where you would be happy to live.

2
00:00:02.913 --> 00:00:05.200
A city that’s not too different from many others.

3
00:00:05.822 --> 00:00:09.068
A place where people live in smaller and bigger communities

4
00:00:09.368 --> 00:00:12.128
they belong to families, circles of friends,

5
00:00:12.297 --> 00:00:14.942
working and school communities, neighbourhoods,

6
00:00:15.127 --> 00:00:17.796
all connected to each other by their regular activities.

7
00:00:18.511 --> 00:00:20.742
These activities, whether they are hobbies

8
00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.251
or responsibilities, need energy.

9
00:00:23.931 --> 00:00:25.443
But where does this energy come from?

10
00:00:26.197 --> 00:00:27.267
For supplying this need,

11
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:30.517
people utilise a wide range of energy sources. 

12
00:00:31.055 --> 00:00:31.824
However,

13
00:00:32.371 --> 00:00:35.002
these sources are not always environmentally friendly.

14
00:00:35.569 --> 00:00:37.205
Producing and consuming energy 

15
00:00:37.338 --> 00:00:39.746
often results in the emission of carbon dioxide

16
00:00:39.824 --> 00:00:41.467
and other greenhouse gasses.

17
00:00:42.145 --> 00:00:44.863
This has a negative impact on the city’s climate

18
00:00:44.932 --> 00:00:47.231
and consequently, on citizen’s lives.

19
00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:51.355
When we look closer, some households are more efficient

20
00:00:51.374 --> 00:00:53.142
in managing their energy consumption. 

21
00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:56.153
Total energy requirements may be different

22
00:00:56.153 --> 00:00:59.055
from household to household, due to many factors

23
00:00:59.513 --> 00:01:01.785
but we all have room for improvement when it comes to

24
00:01:01.908 --> 00:01:05.311
bad practices of excess usage and energy waste.

25
00:01:06.137 --> 00:01:09.267
To limit the impact of climate change, leaders have decided

26
00:01:09.276 --> 00:01:11.730
to take action to try and reduce the emission

27
00:01:11.753 --> 00:01:12.877
of greenhouse gasses.

28
00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:15.846
They have decided to introduce a trading system,

29
00:01:16.433 --> 00:01:19.425
which creates a financial incentive for households

30
00:01:19.426 --> 00:01:20.994
to reduce their emissions.

31
00:01:21.484 --> 00:01:22.975
The system works like this

32
00:01:23.759 --> 00:01:26.562
First, they set a monthly limit, per household,

33
00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:28.971
for the emission of greenhouse gasses,

34
00:01:29.236 --> 00:01:31.671
which will be monitored on a regular basis.

35
00:01:32.549 --> 00:01:34.703
Each household then needs to pay for their emissions

36
00:01:34.768 --> 00:01:36.719
from their monthly carbon emission allowance.

37
00:01:37.278 --> 00:01:40.926
For the sake of simplicity, let’s call these carbon credits.

38
00:01:41.572 --> 00:01:42.995
If one of the households decides

39
00:01:43.097 --> 00:01:44.665
they want to reduce their emissions,

40
00:01:45.051 --> 00:01:46.655
through installing solar panels, 

41
00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:49.064
buying more energy efficient appliances,

42
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.479
switching to biomass energy sources,

43
00:01:51.803 --> 00:01:54.616
or other alternative solutions, they might have some

44
00:01:54.705 --> 00:01:56.450
carbon credits remaining at the end of the month.

45
00:01:57.052 --> 00:01:58.219
They can then sell 

46
00:01:58.309 --> 00:02:01.072
their remaining carbon credits to households that are

47
00:02:01.099 --> 00:02:03.037
about to exceed the limit and need more. 

48
00:02:03.498 --> 00:02:05.155
If a household doesn’t have enough credits

49
00:02:05.185 --> 00:02:08.018
to cover their emissions that month, they would then pay

50
00:02:08.091 --> 00:02:10.627
a special tax, which will be reinvested by the city

51
00:02:10.686 --> 00:02:13.593
to support innovative, eco-friendly solutions,

52
00:02:13.847 --> 00:02:16.696
further reducing the negative impact of the city's emissions.

53
00:02:17.229 --> 00:02:20.330
Over time, carbon credit allowances are reduced,

54
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.636
as more households reduce their emissions

55
00:02:22.953 --> 00:02:24.959
and switch to cleaner energy sources. 

56
00:02:25.672 --> 00:02:29.363
Whilst this city is fictional, the system is not.

57
00:02:29.871 --> 00:02:32.692
In 2005, the European Union established

58
00:02:32.754 --> 00:02:34.387
the Emissions Trading System.

59
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.308
Instead of limiting household emissions, 

60
00:02:37.687 --> 00:02:40.077
the system limits emissions from the power sector,

61
00:02:40.519 --> 00:02:43.274
manufacturing industries, and airlines,

62
00:02:43.556 --> 00:02:46.356
operating in the EU countries as well as Iceland,

63
00:02:46.785 --> 00:02:48.613
Liechtenstein, and Norway.

64
00:02:49.175 --> 00:02:51.969
To find out more, click the link in the description.

